2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle Our algorithm operates on the augmented distribution underlying the proximal sampler of Lee, Shen, and Tian [ 17 ] . For a scale \eta\in(0,1/\beta] , consider the joint law \pi_{\eta}(\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}y)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V(x)-\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\Bigr\}\,\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}y\,. (2.6) The original proximal sampler algorithm performs Gibbs sampling on \pi_{\eta} . Namely, under \pi_{\eta} one may generate the joint state as X\sim\mu\,,\hskip 20.00003ptZ\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)\ \text{independently}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptY=X+\sqrt{\eta}\,Z\,. (2.7) Its X -marginal is \pi_{\eta}^{X}=\mu . Write \pi_{\eta}^{Y} for the Y -marginal, so that \pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\propto e^{-U_{\eta}(y)}\,\mathrm{d}y ; by ( 2.7 ), \pi_{\eta}^{Y}=\mu*\mathcal{N}(0,\eta I) is a Gaussian smoothing of the target. We use superscripts for marginal and conditional laws; in particular, \pi_{\eta}^{Y\mid X=x}=\mathcal{N}(x,\eta I)\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V(x)-\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\Bigr\}\,\mathrm{d}x\,. (2.8) Gibbs sampling alternates exact draws from the two conditional laws in ( 2.8 ). The scale \eta controls the strength of the quadratic coupling potential. Decreasing \eta makes X and Y more tightly coupled and the conditional law of X given Y more localized and better conditioned, which reduces the cost of conditional sampling. The resulting transitions are also more local, however, and generally require more Gibbs sampling iterations. Thus \eta balances the cost of each iteration against the number of iterations. The convergence and query complexity are studied further in [ 6 , 9 , 15 ] . The next proposition collects the basic geometric properties of the augmented distribution used throughout the paper: the curvature and concentration of the conditional law, the curvature of the Y -marginal, and a reflection symmetry of the joint law. Proposition 2.1 (Geometry of the joint law) . The joint law ( 2.6 ) has the following properties. (i) For every y\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , the conditional law \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y} has potential (that is, negative log-density up to an additive constant) V_{y}(x)\coloneqq V(x)+\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\,. (2.9) This conditional potential satisfies (\alpha+\eta^{-1})I\preceq\nabla^{2}V_{y}(x)\preceq(\beta+\eta^{-1})I\,, (2.10) so its condition number is at most (1+\beta\eta)/(1+\alpha\eta)\leq 2 . Consequently, for every smooth f , \Var_{\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}}(f)\leq\frac{\eta}{1+\alpha\eta}\,\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}}\|\nabla f\|^{2}\,, (2.11) and \frac{\eta}{1+\beta\eta}I\preceq\mathrm{Cov}_{\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}}(X)\preceq\frac{\eta}{1+\alpha\eta}I\,. (2.12) (ii) The Y -marginal potential satisfies \frac{\alpha}{1+\alpha\eta}I\preceq\nabla^{2}U_{\eta}(y)\preceq\frac{\beta}{1+\beta\eta}I\,. (2.13) (iii) The state-space reflection \mathsf{U}:(x,y)\mapsto(x,2x-y) (2.14) preserves \pi_{\eta} and is an involution. The Hessian bound in part (i) follows immediately from ( 1.1 ); the Poincaré and covariance bounds then follow from the standard log-concavity facts ( D.6 ) and Lemma D.3 in Appendix D , applied with m=\alpha+\eta^{-1} and L=\beta+\eta^{-1} . The marginal curvature bound in part (ii) is derived in Appendix D , at ( D.10 ). The reflection statement follows directly from ( 2.6 ). We use the standard proximal map convention of Moreau [ 24 ] : \operatorname{prox}_{\eta V}(y)\coloneqq\arg\min_{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\Bigl\{V(x)+\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\Bigr\}\,. (2.15) The minimizer is unique under ( 1.1 ). The objective minimized here is exactly the conditional potential ( 2.9 ), so \operatorname{prox}_{\eta V}(y) is the mode of \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y} ; the conditional draw is in this sense a stochastic analogue of a proximal step, whence the terminology arises. This exact minimizer is used below only in the analysis; the implementable algorithm never evaluates it and does not assume access to a proximal oracle. We use the following conditional sampling primitive, introduced in [ 17 ] . Definition 2.2 (Restricted Gaussian oracle) . For \eta>0 , a restricted Gaussian oracle (RGO) takes input y\in\mathbb{R}^{d} and, using fresh randomness, returns a sample with law \pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y} .